  NaN check cost with unboxed doubles: a Java benchmark (tmp/nan-java/)

  Short answer: HotSpot 25 vectorised none of the three loops, including the plain one. So the throwing check cannot stop vectorisation here, because nothing was vectorised. Both checks cost about 0.3 ns per element over the plain loop, which is 15 to 20 percent. The throwing check and the flag check cost the same.

  Results, median ns per element over 10 timed passes after 20 warm-up passes, 16M elements, counting `a[i] < b[i]` (count 8,387,782 in every run). Each set was run twice; the numbers are run 1 / run 2.
  - (1) plain, default flags: 1.852 / 1.742. With -XX:-UseSuperWord: 1.764 / 1.741.
  - (2) throwing NaN check, default flags: 2.064 / 2.173. With -XX:-UseSuperWord: 2.049 / 2.033.
  - (3) flag NaN check, default flags: 2.030 / 2.036. With -XX:-UseSuperWord: 2.035 / 2.044.

  What it means:
  - Turning SuperWord off changes no loop beyond the noise (at most 0.1 ns per element). So none of the three loops is vectorised by C2 on JDK 25, the plain loop included. The likely reason is that C2 does not vectorise this loop shape: a conditional count (`if (a[i] < b[i]) c++`) as an int reduction over double compares.
  - So the throwing check does not stop vectorisation in this test. There is none to stop. It costs about 0.3 ns per element (2.06 to 2.17 against 1.74 to 1.85), which is about 15 to 20 percent. It comes from the two extra loads and compares and the branch, not from a lost vector loop.
  - The flag variant costs the same as the throwing one (2.03 to 2.04 ns per element). Here it gains nothing, since there is no vector loop for it to keep.
  - At 16M elements a pass reads 256 MB in about 29 ms, about 9 GB/s. The loops are near memory bandwidth, which hides compute differences.
  - C2 compiled every loop (PrintCompilation shows a tier-4 compile of `NanVec::plain` (31 bytes), `NanVec::throwing` (59 bytes) and `NanVec::flagged` (69 bytes), OSR then standard). No method was deoptimised in the timed phase. The only "made not entrant" lines are the usual OSR and tier-3 replacements during warm-up.
  - -XX:+PrintAssembly is unavailable: "Loading hsdis library failed", and there is no hsdis under /usr/lib/jvm. Nothing was installed. TraceSuperWord is a develop flag (debug VM only), and CompileCommand TraceAutoVectorization is not recognised. So the machine code was not read, and the claim that nothing was vectorised rests on the -UseSuperWord timings alone.

  Extra runs, beyond the brief, to test whether loop shape or memory was the cause:
  - NanVecB is NanVec with a branchless count, `c += (a[i] < b[i]) ? 1 : 0`, and an N set by -Dn. I made it with a sed over NanVec.java.
    - 16M, default flags: plain 1.721, throwing 1.908, flagged 1.916 ns per element.
    - 16M, -UseSuperWord: plain 1.696, throwing 1.910, flagged 1.936.
    - 64K, default flags: plain 0.933, throwing 1.587, flagged 1.328.
    - 64K, -UseSuperWord: plain 0.943, throwing 1.330, flagged 1.335.
  - With the branchless count, too, SuperWord makes no difference to the plain loop: it is not vectorised. When the 64K arrays sit in cache, both checks cost about 0.4 ns per element over 0.93, about 40 percent. The 1.587 for the throwing loop with SuperWord on was one run, against 1.330 with it off. Each 64K pass lasts about 0.1 ms, so this may be noise. It is not evidence either way.
  - NanVecS is a control, an add-and-store loop `r[i] = a[i] + b[i]`. At 16M it is memory-bound (1.88 against 1.93 ns per element). At 64K it runs 0.50 to 0.55 against 0.47 to 0.48. At 4K with 200,000 warm-up passes, it runs 0.476 ns per element with SuperWord and 24.2 ns without. That 24.2 is an anomaly: the loop sits in main, and its compilation was not checked. So the control does not separate cleanly, and I give it no weight.
  - Open question for the curator: whether a loop that HotSpot does vectorise (an element-wise store loop, cache-resident, run in a method of its own) loses its vector form when a throwing NaN check is added. This benchmark could not show it, because none of the specified loops vectorised.

  Machine:
  - nproc 4. CPU: Intel(R) Xeon(R) Processor @ 2.10GHz, with sse4_2, avx, avx2 and avx512f.
  - Load average 0.00 to 0.23 before the runs. No other work ran.
  - JVM: openjdk version "25.0.4.1" 2026-08-18, OpenJDK Runtime Environment (build 25.0.4.1+1-1-24.04.4-Ubuntu), OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM (mixed mode, sharing).

  Source: /home/user/fortress/tmp/nan-java/NanVec.java, whole:

  ```java
  import java.util.Arrays;

  public class NanVec {
      static final int N = 1 << 24;          // 16,777,216 elements
      static final int WARM = 20, TIMED = 10;

      // (1) plain comparison
      static int plain(double[] a, double[] b) {
          int c = 0;
          for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
              if (a[i] < b[i]) c++;
          }
          return c;
      }

      // (2) NaN check that throws, as the Fortress helper would
      static int throwing(double[] a, double[] b) {
          int c = 0;
